      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Tonsillectomy is a common operation for the otolaryngologist. There has been a discrepancy in recommending prescribing a liquid based or solid medication for post-tonsillectomy patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To compare the pain scores, adverse effects and complications between the post-tonsillectomy patients who were given liquid medication in comparison with patients who were given non-restricted medication."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHOD",
          "content": "Patients with chronic hypertrophic tonsillitis who underwent tonsillectomy were recruited. In the control group, patients were given liquid medication. The experimental group was given a non-restricted form of medication. Pain scores, adverse effects and complications and patient satisfaction data were collected."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Twenty-six patients were enrolled. The pain score difference between the 2 groups at 4 hours was -0.23 (95% CI -1.57 to 1.11, p = 0.73) and 0.15 (95% CI -0.77 to 1.08, p = 0.73) at 72 hours. There was no statistically significant difference between the early and late complications between the control group and the experimental group (p > 0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "There was no statistical difference in the pain scores, adverse effects and complications between groups. There is no necessity to restrict patients to liquid medication."
        }
      ]},
